<h2>What I actually do</h2>

<p>
    I am the only QA engineer on this digital health platform, which reaches
    {% metric "monthly_users" %} in {% metric "languages" %} languages across
    {% metric "countries" %} countries. My job is the evidence layer underneath a claim nobody can
    make by inspection: that a generated answer is safe to send.
</p>

<p>
    That breaks down into three things. Golden datasets and judge design, which is how we decide
    what good looks like before a release. Production monitoring, which is how we find out we were
    wrong about it. And the test infrastructure underneath both: Playwright, API and contract
    suites, and the CI they run in.
</p>

{% architectureNote "long" %}

<h2>How I got here</h2>

<p>
    I joined as an intern in January 2022 and converted to full time that May. The project had no
    QA process, so the first year was building one from nothing: templates, severity standards,
    the first UI automation suite, the first API suite, the first CI integration. It is unglamorous
    work and it is the reason I have opinions about test infrastructure that are not borrowed.
</p>

<p>
    AI testing started in September 2023 with intent classification and conversational flows. The
    real shift was Q1 2025, when I built the platform's first LLM-as-a-judge. I then found that
    it was grading answers produced by the same model family that generated them. Everything I have
    built since traces back to that, because the question it raised is the interesting one:
    <a href="{{ '/work/llm-judge-independence/' | url }}">what makes an evaluation independent
    enough to be worth anything?</a>
</p>

<p>
    QA on the project consolidated to a single engineer in October 2025. I have been Scrum Master
    since April 2025, and I was promoted to Senior QA Automation Engineer on 1 June 2026. The scope
    did not change at the promotion; the title caught up with it.
</p>

<h2>What I am like to work with</h2>

<p>
    I write down what went wrong. Every case study on this site has a section on what I would do
    differently, and at least one item in it is still open. This is not self-deprecation. It is
    the only way I know to make the rest of the claims checkable. If someone tells you their
    systems have no limitations, they have not measured them.
</p>

<p>
    I am careful about attribution. Two of the largest performance improvements in the pipeline I
    work on were other engineers' work; I built the measurement that attributed them. Where that
    is true on this site, it says so on the same line as the number, not in a footnote.
</p>

<p>
    I hedge in proportion to the evidence and no further. {% metric "golden_dataset_india_mh" %} is
    a count, so it is stated as a count. A self-tracked estimate says it is self-tracked. A replay
    projection says it never happened in production.
</p>

